;;; Two owed families on one relation: the obligations rung
;;; recorded a strict superset of a model, chosen by name order, at **default
;;; flags**. Regression `owed-seats` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, two chairs. `total-owed` says every guest has a chair;
;;; `surjective-owed` says every chair has a guest. Nothing else.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The two matchings, `{(seats Ann C1), (seats Bob C2)}` and `{(seats Ann
;;; C2), (seats Bob C1)}`, discharge both families. Whether a state that seats
;;; Ann on **both** chairs and Bob on one — owing nothing — is a third model is
;;; Q-M1e2.1's reading:
;;;
;;;   minimal  k = 2 — a requirement is discharged by a witness, a complete
;;;            node leaves the frontier, and a second witness is
;;;            `functional`'s question, not `total`'s. Not taken; it is R's
;;;            minimal line, which `ein solve --minimal` answers.
;;;   literal  k = 7 — every seating that owes nothing: the two matchings,
;;;            the four triples and all four facts. **It holds**: k = |R|
;;;            (Q-M1e2.1, Q-M1e2.7); a bare `ein solve` answers it.
;;;
;;; **Never 3**, and never a set that changes when a constant is renamed.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   lattice  Ambiguity k = 3, `exhausted = true`: the two matchings and
;;;            `{(seats Ann C1), (seats Ann C2), (seats Bob C1)}` — a superset
;;;            of a recorded model. `apriori.rs` said every (k−1)-subset of a
;;;            joined candidate was in `a_prev` *by construction*; the prefix
;;;            join guaranteed only the two parents, so a superset of a node
;;;            that left the frontier as complete was joined anyway — and only
;;;            the supersets the name-sorted join happened to build.
;;;   tree     (`EIN_TRAVERSAL=tree`) k = 6, a lower bound: the matchings and
;;;            all four triples.
;;;
;;; `owed-seats-renamed.ein` is this file with Ann renamed Zed and nothing
;;; else; its third model was a *different* triple. Both rows are the walk's
;;; two traversals; a bare `ein solve` answers the seven states in both files.
;;;
;;;
(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed surjective-owed))
(relation is-a   T T :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation seats  Guest Chair :why "{?3} sits on {?4}")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(is-a C1 Chair)  (is-a C2 Chair)
(total-owed      seats is-a)
(surjective-owed seats is-a)
(query :goal (seats ?g ?c))
